<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vladimir the Bold</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1353">1353</a>–<a href="/years/1410">1410</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Vladimir Andreyevich of Serpukhov · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vladimir the Bold, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpukhov">Serpukhov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borovsk">Borovsk</a>, was a cousin of Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and his leading general. He commanded the reserve regiment that decided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kulikovo">Battle of Kulikovo</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> in 1380, and he defended Moscow during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edigu">Edigu</a>'s raid of 1408.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-bold.jpg" alt="Vladimir the Bold" width="320" height="499" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Стенопись Архангельского собора Московского Кремля · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9A%D0%BD%D1%8F%D0%B7%D1%8C_%D0%92%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%B4%D0%B8%D0%BC%D0%B8%D1%80_%D0%A5%D1%80%D0%B0%D0%B1%D1%80%D1%8B%D0%B9,_%D0%A4%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%B0_%D0%90%D1%80%D1%85%D0%B0%D0%BD%D0%B3%D0%B5%D0%BB%D1%8C%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B3%D0%BE_%D1%81%D0%BE%D0%B1%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%B0.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vladimir Andreyevich was born on 15 July 1353, the son of Andrei Ivanovich, Prince of Novgorod, and a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-i-of-moscow">Ivan Kalita</a>. At the age of five he inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpukhov">Serpukhov</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borovsk">Borovsk</a> and a third part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> from his brother. Because his cousin Dmitry of Moscow was also a child, both princes were tutored by Metropolitan Alexis, who arranged a treaty binding Vladimir to loyalty to his Muscovite cousin. His byname refers to his many military exploits in Dmitry's wars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Under that treaty Vladimir helped Dmitry fight Tver in 1375, the Livonian Knights in 1379, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> in 1385 and the Republic of Novgorod in 1392. He married a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1371 but still supported Dmitry against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>. In 1374 he built the first oaken kremlin at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpukhov">Serpukhov</a>, and in 1377 he sacked the Severian towns of Trubchevsk and Starodub. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> invaded two years after Kulikovo, Vladimir defeated his force near Volokolamsk.</p><p>It is not clear why he quarrelled with his cousin in 1388. They made peace the same year, but after Dmitry's death and the accession of his son Vasily I, Vladimir had to leave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpukhov">Serpukhov</a> for Torzhok. A year later he returned and made a treaty with Vasily that gave him Volokolamsk and Rzhev, which he later exchanged for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorodets">Gorodets</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uglich">Uglich</a> and Kozelsk while giving up his claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murom">Murom</a> and Tarusa.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died in 1410 and was buried in the Archangel Cathedral. His seven sons continued the line of the princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpukhov">Serpukhov</a> until 1456. His granddaughter Maria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borovsk">Borovsk</a> married Vasily II and was the mother of Ivan the Great, who drove the last princes of Serpukhov into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. The last of Vladimir's male-line descendants died in 1521.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vladimir the Bold” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Andrei's son <b>Vladimir the Bold</b>, cousin of Dmitry Donskoy, was Moscow's leading general.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-serpukhov">Principality of Serpukhov</a></li></ul></section>
